<section class="hero wrap"><div class="wordmark" aria-hidden="true">jev logs<span>_</span></div><div class="hero-grid"><div class="hero-copy"><p class="eyebrow">OPEN SOURCE · OPENTELEMETRY · TYPESCRIPT</p><h1>Keep your logs.<br/>Spend on<br/><em>the signal.</em></h1><p class="lead">Your LLM doesn’t need every health check.<br/>Let Jev find the logs worth thinking about.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/guide/">Start building <span>↗</span></a><a class="text-link" href="#savings">Calculate your savings ↓</a></div><a class="savings-proof" href="#savings"><strong>87.1% lower modeled LLM spend</strong><span>$1,000 → $129.40 / month in our 1M-log example.<br/>Assumes 10% need deeper analysis. Explore the math ↓</span></a><p class="launch-note"><code>npx jevlogs</code> — try the offline demo</p><p class="launch-note">v0.1.1 · MIT licensed · npm install jevlogs</p></div>
<div class="demo"><div class="demo-top"><span><span class="dot"></span> log pipeline</span><span class="badge">ILLUSTRATIVE DEMO</span></div><div class="pipeline"><span>OpenTelemetry</span><span>→</span><strong>Jev</strong><span>→</span><span>LLM analysis</span></div><div class="demo-heading"><span>INCOMING EVENT</span><span>VALUE / 100</span></div><div class="log-row" data-value="0" data-route="Retain only"><div><span class="severity">INFO</span><code>GET /health → 200</code><small>api-gateway · routine heartbeat</small></div><span class="score">—</span></div><div class="log-row" data-value="25" data-route="Retain only"><div><span class="severity">DEBUG</span><code>Cache hit: product:482</code><small>catalog · expected operation</small></div><span class="score">—</span></div><div class="log-row important" data-value="100" data-route="Analyze"><div><span class="severity error">ERROR</span><code>Payment capture failed</code><small>checkout · protected by severity</small></div><span class="score">—</span></div><div class="log-row" data-value="75" data-route="Analyze"><div><span class="severity warn">WARN</span><code>Connection pool near capacity</code><small>database · investigate degradation</small></div><span class="score">—</span></div><div class="demo-bottom"><p id="demo-status" aria-live="polite">4 logs preserved. Find what deserves analysis.</p><button id="run-demo" type="button">Run demo ↗</button></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap integrations"><span class="micro">FITS YOUR EXISTING STACK</span><strong>OpenTelemetry</strong><span>+</span><strong>Jev by TypeSafe</strong><span>+</span><strong>Vercel AI SDK</strong><span class="end-note">Your exporters. Your infrastructure.</span></div>
<section id="how" class="wrap section"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">01 / A SMALLER FIRST DECISION</p><h2>Think less about noise.<br/><em>Learn more from failures.</em></h2><p>Put a fast decision model before your reasoning model. Keep your observability pipeline, and choose what deserves a deeper look.</p></div><div class="features"><article><span class="number">01</span><h3>Give every log a value.</h3><p>Jev grades diagnostic value on a 0–100 rubric, assigns urgency, and estimates whether further investigation is useful.</p></article><article><span class="number">02</span><h3>Keep the uncertain ones.</h3><p>Only confidently low-value events can skip analysis. Errors and protected records always pass through. A timeout preserves the record.</p></article><article><span class="number">03</span><h3>Make fewer expensive calls.</h3><p>Annotate first. Then enable routing on a separate LLM branch while your existing telemetry archive keeps everything.</p></article></div><div class="launch-story"><img src="/images/introducing-jevlogs.png" alt="Introducing Jev Logs: our relaxed robot mascot holding a blue log card. jevlogs.com" width="1024" height="1024" loading="lazy"/><div><p class="eyebrow">ONE SMALL DECISION BEFORE A BIGGER BILL</p><h3>How Jev Logs uses Jev</h3><p>The TypeScript exporter sends a redacted copy of the log body and severity through the Vercel AI SDK. One Jev evaluation asks three questions: diagnostic value, priority, and whether investigation is actionable.</p><p>The SDK maps those answers into an analysis recommendation. ERROR/FATAL and protected records bypass evaluation and stay eligible for analysis. Uncertain decisions and failures also pass through.</p><p>Your archive remains on its own branch. Only confidently low-value records skip the analysis branch. You connect the downstream LLM; Jev Logs supplies the routing decision.</p><a class="text-link" href="/guide/">See the OpenTelemetry integration ↗</a></div></div></section>

<section id="use-cases" class="wrap section capabilities">
  <div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">WHAT YOU CAN DO TODAY</p><h2>From a local log file<br/><em>to your production pipeline.</em></h2><p>Start in the terminal, add a decision to your application, or enrich the OpenTelemetry logs you already collect. One npm package, three entry points.</p></div>
  <div class="features">
    <article><span class="number">TERMINAL</span><h3>Try it in seconds.</h3><p>Run <code>npx jevlogs</code> for an offline walkthrough. Add <code>--live</code> and your Gateway key to evaluate the sample logs with real Jev.</p><a class="capability-link" href="/guide/#1-try-the-cli">CLI quick start ↗</a></article>
    <article><span class="number">TEXT + JSONL</span><h3>Inspect a log snapshot.</h3><p>Read a file or pipe finite input through stdin. Get scores and priorities in the terminal, or JSONL decisions for your scripts. Your original file stays intact.</p><a class="capability-link" href="/guide/#accepted-input">Input formats ↗</a></article>
    <article><span class="number">TYPESCRIPT</span><h3>Prioritize your queue.</h3><p>Call <code>triage()</code> before deeper analysis. Your application gets value, priority, probability, and a routing recommendation to act on.</p><a class="capability-link" href="/guide/#2-use-the-typescript-api">Standalone API ↗</a></article>
    <article><span class="number">OPENTELEMETRY</span><h3>See signal in your backend.</h3><p>Wrap a compatible exporter to add <code>jev.*</code> attributes. Keep every record and explore the recommendations in your existing observability tools.</p><a class="capability-link" href="/guide/#3-annotate-your-opentelemetry-logs">Annotation setup ↗</a></article>
    <article><span class="number">SELECTIVE ANALYSIS</span><h3>Keep an archive. Filter a branch.</h3><p>Leave your archive on its own processor. Add Jev to a separate analysis queue so only confidently low-value records skip expensive reasoning.</p><a class="capability-link" href="/guide/#4-add-a-separate-analysis-branch">Two-branch example ↗</a></article>
    <article><span class="number">CONTROL</span><h3>Set the rules for your data.</h3><p>Protect audit events, add your own redactor, and tune the bypass threshold. Provider failures and ambiguous decisions stay eligible for analysis.</p><a class="capability-link" href="/guide/#5-protect-records-and-redact-context">Protection and redaction ↗</a></article>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="workflow-strip"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">YOUR FIRST FIVE MINUTES</p><div class="workflow-steps"><div><b>01 · Try</b><code>npx jevlogs</code><p>Offline samples. No key required.</p></div><div><b>02 · Evaluate</b><code>npx jevlogs --live --file app.log</code><p>Real Jev. Gateway key required.</p></div><div><b>03 · Integrate</b><code>npm install jevlogs</code><p>A typed decision in your own code.</p></div></div><p class="small">Live mode sends redacted bodies to Gateway / TypeSafe and incurs provider charges. The CLI is a finite batch tool: 20 records by default, up to 100. It waits for EOF when reading stdin.</p></div></section>
<section class="why"><div class="wrap why-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">BUILT FOR DECISIONS, NOT PARAGRAPHS</p><h2>Why Jev?<br/><em>The math is different.</em></h2></div><div><div class="price"><strong>$0.042</strong><span>per million input tokens<br/>TypeSafe’s published launch price</span></div><p>Typed choices, rubric scores, and probabilities in one request. No generated explanation needed for a routing decision.</p><div class="why-details"><span><b>Parallel questions</b>Shared log context</span><span><b>Free output</b>At TypeSafe launch pricing</span><span><b>Explicit uncertainty</b>Keep ambiguous events</span></div><p class="small">Gateway lists $0.04/M; billing may differ. Jev is early access. Provider speed benchmarks are not Jev Logs benchmarks. Typed outputs can still make incorrect decisions. <a href="https://typesafe.ai/blog/introducing-system-one-models-and-jev">Read TypeSafe’s announcement ↗</a></p></div></div></section>
<section id="savings" class="wrap section"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">02 / LESS NOISE. A SMALLER BILL.</p><h2>A smaller analysis bill.<br/><em>Every log still kept.</em></h2><p>A transparent estimate, not a benchmark. Adjust the fraction of logs still sent to your LLM, including errors, uncertain decisions, and failures.</p></div><p class="pricing-note">Example baseline: <a href="https://vercel.com/ai-gateway/models/gpt-4.1">GPT-4.1 at $2/M input and $8/M output</a>. Jev uses <a href="https://typesafe.ai/blog/introducing-system-one-models-and-jev">TypeSafe’s $0.042/M input launch rate</a>. Prices checked September 16, 2026. The routing fraction and 400-token question overhead are assumptions; measure yours.</p><div class="calculator"><form id="cost-form"><label>Logs per month<input name="logs" type="number" min="0" max="1000000000000" value="1000000" required/></label><label>Input tokens per log<input name="tokensPerLog" type="number" min="0" value="300" required/></label><label>LLM input · $ / 1M tokens<input name="llmInputPerMillion" type="number" min="0" step="0.001" value="2" required/></label><label>LLM output · $ / 1M tokens<input name="llmOutputPerMillion" type="number" min="0" step="0.001" value="8" required/></label><label>Output tokens per log<input name="outputTokensPerLog" type="number" min="0" value="50" required/></label><label>Logs still analyzed · %<input name="retainedPercent" type="number" min="0" max="100" value="10" required/></label><label>Jev input · $ / 1M tokens<input name="jevInputPerMillion" type="number" min="0" step="0.001" value="0.042" required/></label><label>Jev question / context overhead · tokens<input name="questionTokensPerLog" type="number" min="0" value="400" required/></label></form><div class="estimate" aria-live="polite"><p class="micro">ESTIMATED MONTHLY SAVINGS</p><strong id="saved">$870.60</strong><span id="percent">87.1% less analysis spend</span><div class="cost-line"><span>Analyze everything</span><b id="baseline">$1,000.00</b></div><div class="bar baseline-bar"></div><div class="cost-line"><span>Jev + selected LLM calls</span><b id="with-jev">$129.40</b></div><div class="bar jev-bar" id="jev-bar"></div><p class="small">Includes Jev input and question overhead, plus selected LLM input and output. Excludes storage, ingestion, hosting, retries, discounts, and prompt caching. Assumes equal average log sizes; savings depend on your workload.</p></div></div><div class="scenarios"><h3>How much noise can you skip?</h3><p>Same 1M-log workload and GPT-4.1 rates. Jev triage adds $29.40; the rest depends on how many records still need analysis.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Logs still analyzed</th><th>Jev + analysis</th><th>Modeled reduction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>100%</td><td>$1,029.40</td><td>2.94% cost increase</td></tr><tr><td>50%</td><td>$529.40</td><td>47.06%</td></tr><tr><td>25%</td><td>$279.40</td><td>72.06%</td></tr><tr><td>10%</td><td>$129.40</td><td>87.06%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="pricing-note">Already using a smaller model? At <a href="https://vercel.com/ai-gateway/models/gpt-4.1-mini">GPT-4.1 mini’s $0.40/M input and $1.60/M output</a>, the same 10% scenario is $200 → $49.40: a modeled 75.3% reduction. This compares costs, not accuracy. Annotation alone saves no analysis calls; enable filtering on your separate analysis branch after evaluating incident recall.</p></div></section>
<section class="wrap section install"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / SMALL INTEGRATION. REAL CONTROL.</p><h2>Your pipeline.<br/><em>One new layer.</em></h2><p>Install with npm install jevlogs. Try npx jevlogs for the offline demo, or npx jevlogs --live with your Gateway key. Then wrap your existing OpenTelemetry exporter.</p><a class="button" href="/guide/">Read the quick start <span>↗</span></a></div><div class="code-panel"><div class="code-heading"><span>telemetry.ts</span><button type="button" id="copy-code">Copy</button></div><pre><code id="install-code">{snippet}</code></pre><p class="small code-note">Node.js 22+ · AI SDK 7.0.105 · OTel SDK Logs 0.222.0</p></div></section>

<section class="wrap section faq" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
  <div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">BEFORE YOU INTEGRATE</p><h2 id="faq-heading">Know what the first<br/><em>version can do.</em></h2></div>
  <details><summary>Does installing Jev Logs automatically lower my bill?</summary><p>No. Annotation mode exports every record with extra attributes. Savings become possible when your consumer honors the routing recommendation, or when you enable a separate analysis-only branch. If every record still goes to an LLM, triage adds cost.</p></details>
  <details><summary>What do value, priority, and probability mean?</summary><p>Value is a diagnostic rubric scaled to 0–100. Priority is low, normal, high, or critical. Actionable probability estimates whether deeper investigation would be useful. These are separate signals; the value is not dollars or confidence.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Which records can skip deeper analysis?</summary><p>Only records with low priority, value at most 25, and actionable probability below 0.1 by default. Errors and explicitly protected records bypass the model and remain eligible for analysis. Uncertain or unavailable evaluations also stay on that path.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Does it replace my log backend or run root-cause analysis?</summary><p>No. Keep your existing storage, instrumentation, exporters, and analysis model. This version provides triage decisions and an OpenTelemetry Logs exporter wrapper. It does not include a hosted dashboard, Collector plugin, durable queue, trace sampling, continuous CLI tailing, or root-cause explanations.</p></details>
  <details><summary>What leaves my application?</summary><p>The standard evaluator sends the log body and severity after redaction. The default redactor covers common labeled secrets, Bearer tokens, and email addresses. Add domain-specific rules as needed. Redaction applies to the model-bound copy, not your original exported record.</p></details>
  <details><summary>How do I start safely with my own workload?</summary><p>Try synthetic records, then use annotation mode on a bounded workload. Compare decisions against labeled incidents and inspect false negatives. Choose thresholds before enabling filtering, and review a sample of bypassed events. Live accuracy and savings have not been independently validated for this project.</p></details>
  <a class="capability-link" href="/guide/">Full reference, examples, and troubleshooting ↗</a>
</section>
